# Covers: whenever (cond) body — a reactive LOOP.  The body fires on the
#         rising edge and re-fires as soon as it finishes, for as long as
#         the condition still holds; the loop ends when the condition
#         goes false, not when some number of scheduler safepoints have
#         gone by.
#
# The body here takes the condition down one step per run, so the number
# of runs is a property of the program (three, for x = 8 down to 5) rather
# than of how often the scheduler happened to look.
#
# HISTORY.  This fixture used to pin `fired == 3` for a body that never
# touched its condition, and the 3 was the number of safepoints the old
# core's per-pass evaluation happened to reach.  Under the ratified
# semantics that program does not terminate at all — which is what a
# `whenever` whose body cannot falsify its condition means, exactly as a
# `while (true)` does.  See reactive/at/at_rising_edge.chk for the
# edge-triggered contrast: `at` fires once per false->true transition
# whatever the body does.

var x = 0
var fired = 0
whenever (x > 5) { fired = fired + 1; x = x - 1 }
var t1 = function() { x = 8 }; t1()
fired
x
[00000000] 0
[00000000] 0
[00000000] nil
[00000000] 8
[00000000] 3
[00000000] 5
